  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nPartly cloudy skies this afternoon with a warm front lifting\nnortheast across our area. Breezy winds gusting to 20-25 mph\nbehind the front out of the south-southwest helping\ntemperatures to warm into the upper 80s to low 90s this \nafternoon. Looking at the upper-level pattern, we have broad \nridging over the Central US with a trough over the Inter- \nMountain West. A subtle shortwave has led to the development of \nour surface low currently positioned over north-central Nebraska\nnear the SD state line. At the same time, another weak surface \nlow is developing over southeastern Colorado. \n\nThis evening we're monitoring the Low over north-central\nNebraska, expected to pull the cold front south but hanging up\non the developing system to our southwest. This will position a\nstationary front somewhere southwest to northeast along or just\nnorth of I-80 as the upper-level wave moves through. We expect\nscattered storms to trigger along this boundary around 7PM this\nevening and track northeast along the front exiting around\n11PM-12AM. Model soundings show a fairly strong surface stable\nlayer with an exorbitant amount of MUCAPE aloft (3000-4500\nJ/kg). Dry air entrainment will likely limit the ability to\nutilize that amount of energy. While shear is fairly good in the\nlow-levels near the frontal boundary, this is mostly generated\nby the enhancement of the LLJ with bulk of convection occurring\nabove this layer. This means storms may struggle to organize\ninto anything strong to severe. What we may see, though, if we\ncan get a couple of stronger storms to develop, is potential \nfor localized strong to severe winds. As storms collapse, we \nmay see downbursts attempt to push through the surface stable \nlayer. If they can punch through, this would lead to a couple \nof isolated bursts of strong to severe wind. That's probably our\nmain hazard possible with any of these storms this evening.\n\nOvernight tonight we see the second weak low pressure center\nride up the stationary front into central Nebraska bringing a\nbroader complex of showers with a few rumbles of thunder into \nour area early Saturday morning. By the time this system \narrives, environmental instability will have significantly waned\nreducing the chance of thunderstorms with this second round.\nMore of what this will do is pick up the stationary front and\npush it south across far southeast Nebraska into south-central\nIowa. Models have been shifting the position of where this\nfrontal boundary will stall on Saturday, shifting the area of\npotential severe weather Saturday afternoon and evening as\nanother shortwave moves through. Latest trends have been to\nbring it farther south, keeping the stronger storms south across\neastern Kansas into northwest Missouri. For this reason, SPC has\nshifted our marginal and slight risks for severe storms south,\nout of our area. We'll still watch for some potential this \nshifts back north, but as of now I'd expect Saturday evening to\nstay quiet across our area. \n\nGoing into the latter half of the weekend, we see the broad\nridge over the Central US shift east with the upper-level trough\nmoving in from the west. This will mean periodic chances for\nshowers and weak storms Sunday through Monday with a cooler air\nmass in place. Highs on Sunday will only warm into the low-to-\nmid 60s for most of our area, and Monday many may only peak at\n60 degrees. \n\nBeyond Monday we see ridging build back up behind the transient\nupper-level trough with return flow bringing back in drier \nconditions for Tuesday and Wednesday. We see the belt of \nmoisture advection from the Gulf shift east back over our area \nfor the latter half of the week next week. This will mean more \nchances for showers and storms Thursday and Friday though \ntemperatures look to remain relatively cool with highs still\nonly in the mid 60s to low 70s.",
